{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "262",
    "case_summary": "A 55-year-old white woman with a history of diverticulosis and colonic polyps presented with new-onset flulike symptoms followed by low-grade fever, facial crusted plaques, and 4 weeks of bloody and watery diarrhea. Her facial lesions initially presented as ill-defined pink patches over her cheeks and red papules on her forehead. Over the next 4 days, the lesions spread to her chest, abdomen, back, and left buttock, and a single papule appeared on the right lower extremity. Her low-grade fevers and diarrhea persisted throughout this 4-day period.\n\nIn the emergency department, she was febrile and tachycardic. Physical findings included:- 6 × 7-cm well-demarcated vegetative crusted plaques on the bilateral cheeks- Several discrete, round, 1- to 2-cm crusted plaques on the temple- Discrete pustules, vesicles, and umbilicated papules on cheeks and temple- Lesions on chest, abdomen, back, and left buttock- Painful subcutaneous nodules over lower extremities- Polyarthralgia\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Mild leukocytosis with normal differential- Mildly elevated transaminase and alkaline phosphatase levels- Negative wound culture- Blood cultures showed no growth- Negative PCR for herpes simplex virus- Negative direct fluorescent antibody assay for varicella-zoster virusPathology:- Forehead lesion biopsy: Intradermal neutrophilic inflammation with ulceration and intracorneal neutrophils. Special stains negative for microorganisms- Right thigh biopsy: Septal panniculitis with Miescher radial granulomas- Colonoscopy: Moderate inflammation, edema, erosions, and pseudopolyps in the sigmoid colonImages:Image 1 Title: Clinical Appearance of Facial Neutrophilic DermatosisImage 1 Description: A, A 6 × 7-cm, well-demarcated, vegetative crusted plaque on the left cheek and a discrete, round, 1- to 2-cm crusted plaque on the foreheadImage 2 Title: Histopathological Findings of Facial Neutrophilic DermatosisImage 2 Description: Shave biopsy specimen from forehead lesion showing diffuse intradermal neutrophilic infiltrate with central ulceration",
